00:02:51.809 --> 00:02:53.118
I am Raj Rajeshirke.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.167
With me, I have Pranali Bane.

00:02:55.572 --> 00:03:01.031
Uh, we represent BarrierBreak, and
today we'll be talking about one of the

00:03:01.031 --> 00:03:03.777
most important topics in accessibility.

00:03:04.134 --> 00:03:09.761
Generally, people do not regularly
think about focus management, uh, but

00:03:09.768 --> 00:03:14.952
yeah, that is one of the primary pillars
of accessibility, and that can affect

00:03:14.963 --> 00:03:18.923
users' experience at a, a higher level.

00:03:19.587 --> 00:03:26.007
So we will be, uh, discussing the topic
in three parts, basically focusing

00:03:26.007 --> 00:03:31.046
on visibility, predictability, and
control as three separate sections.

00:03:31.382 --> 00:03:36.013
Uh, we'll understand why
focus management matters.

00:03:36.361 --> 00:03:40.588
Uh, then we'll show you some demos
on live websites, and then we'll

00:03:40.614 --> 00:03:42.063
conclude our session for today.

00:03:42.983 --> 00:03:48.156
Basically starting out with the
agenda, why focus management matters.

00:03:48.683 --> 00:03:51.279
So let's start with the case.

00:03:51.675 --> 00:03:56.245
So we are basically building a case
out here trying to understand, uh,

00:03:56.248 --> 00:03:58.450
why and how focus management matters.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:02.636
Uh, does the user interface
make sense to everyone?

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:06.333
Like, can users track
themselves on the website?

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.656
Will users be able to
predict where they end up?

00:04:10.428 --> 00:04:13.664
These are some simple questions that
come to mind when you think about

00:04:13.672 --> 00:04:21.202
focus management, but this can create
a huge impact when done right and when

00:04:21.202 --> 00:04:23.171
implemented in the correct fashion.

00:04:23.672 --> 00:04:29.385
So looking at, uh, mouse users, how
would a mouse user be navigating?

00:04:29.655 --> 00:04:33.504
They would definitely be clicking
controls, and they would expect

00:04:33.504 --> 00:04:36.376
a output instantaneously, right?

00:04:36.962 --> 00:04:42.543
Somebody who is primarily using a keyboard
may not be able to, uh, do the same or

00:04:42.557 --> 00:04:44.837
have the same luxury as a mouse user.

00:04:45.433 --> 00:04:48.498
So what is expected out of that scenario?

00:04:48.948 --> 00:04:53.970
Users using keyboard, uh, as well as
screen reader plus keyboard combinations

00:04:54.238 --> 00:04:58.366
should be able to tell what the
user interface is, w- what they're

00:04:58.366 --> 00:05:03.474
interacting with, where they are
currently on the webpage, and whether

00:05:03.513 --> 00:05:05.486
they can predict where they'll end up.

00:05:06.785 --> 00:05:13.304
So we'll, uh, start with our
presentation in the three pillar format.

00:05:13.405 --> 00:05:18.493
Uh, first pillar would be visibility,
second would be predictability,

00:05:18.822 --> 00:05:20.397
and third would be control.

00:05:20.727 --> 00:05:27.298
So visibility means can users tell
currently where they stand or where

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.367
on the page they are right now?

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:36.164
Predictability can be defined as
can users predict or forecast where

00:05:36.185 --> 00:05:39.563
they'll end up when they're performing
certain actions on the webpage?

00:05:40.343 --> 00:05:40.618
Yep.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.299
Again, the third is control.

00:05:43.527 --> 00:05:48.012
Can users control what happens when
they are trying to interact with a

00:05:48.016 --> 00:05:52.599
control, something like a dialogue,
something like a pop-up that appears?

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.510
Are users able to manage
and recover from that?

00:05:57.019 --> 00:05:58.941
Uh, that we'll be looking at.

00:05:59.719 --> 00:06:04.024
Again, together these three
principles will create a smooth,

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.540
understandable navigation experience.

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:12.962
Uh, even if one of these are missing,
users can become qu-quickly confused,

00:06:13.044 --> 00:06:18.216
and they might face problems navigating
and understanding the webpage and the

00:06:18.218 --> 00:06:19.477
overall structure of the webpages.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:25.648
I can give you a few examples which
turned out a visible focus indicator

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:29.905
without a predictable movement
still creates confusion, right?

00:06:30.177 --> 00:06:35.238
So this is a predictable movement
without a clear, uh, control where

00:06:35.246 --> 00:06:40.798
users can navigate, uh, without trap
is again a problem for users who are

00:06:40.799 --> 00:06:42.837
navigating primarily using a keyboard.

00:06:43.537 --> 00:06:48.553
Controls without, uh, visibility leaves,
uh, users unsure of their location.

00:06:48.962 --> 00:06:55.422
And of course, uh, sometimes accessibility
is not only about making sure that

00:06:55.448 --> 00:07:01.486
controls are accessible or available to
keyboard users, it is also about creating

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:08.585
a meaningful journey, uh, which users can
understand and predictably recreate once

00:07:08.602 --> 00:07:10.798
they are interacting with the controls.

00:07:11.417 --> 00:07:14.784
So let's start with the first
pillar, which is visibility.

00:07:15.237 --> 00:07:19.787
Keyboard users primarily use a focus
indicator to understand where they

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:25.321
are on the webpage and where they
will end up by, you know, navigating

00:07:25.321 --> 00:07:27.131
using Tab and Shift+Tab keys.

00:07:27.816 --> 00:07:33.302
So the primary indicator, uh,
is expected that is Even a plain

00:07:33.342 --> 00:07:38.795
outline, a black border of simple
one pixel would be enough at times.

00:07:39.315 --> 00:07:43.427
Uh, what, uh, common mistakes
do, uh, developers make?

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:50.008
They sometimes remove this focus
indicators, and that can affect

00:07:50.046 --> 00:07:54.663
the user accessibility or user
experience at a much higher scale.

00:07:55.205 --> 00:08:00.889
For example, users are not able to
tell where I am, or users are not able

00:08:00.927 --> 00:08:05.270
to identify with the indicator that
the developer has presented them with.

00:08:05.908 --> 00:08:10.995
Uh, users are not able to, at times,
see the indicator clearly, or the

00:08:10.996 --> 00:08:13.305
indicator may fail color contrast.

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:18.218
So these are a few of the scenarios that,
uh, we can think of when users are trying

00:08:18.218 --> 00:08:21.031
to navigate using a keyboard primarily.

00:08:22.017 --> 00:08:25.394
A few of the examples that,
uh, we have on screen.

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:29.823
So this example, we have
a lack of focus indicator.

00:08:30.267 --> 00:08:31.833
Uh, we have two screenshots.

00:08:31.856 --> 00:08:36.966
One shows the control without a focus
indicator, and the other one shows a

00:08:36.966 --> 00:08:42.615
control with a basic black border or
outline which acts as a focus indicator.

00:08:43.071 --> 00:08:47.778
So lack of focus indicator, again,
users can't track themselves.

00:08:48.079 --> 00:08:53.352
Uh, users cannot predict whether they
are interacting witH1 control or not.

00:08:53.806 --> 00:08:55.098
They have to take a guess.

00:08:55.128 --> 00:08:57.344
They have to repeat their
actions again and again.

00:08:58.123 --> 00:09:02.780
With a basic focus indicator, such as,
uh, highlighted in this scenario on

00:09:02.783 --> 00:09:08.593
the image, uh, we have three options,
uh, under filter by section, color

00:09:08.606 --> 00:09:11.332
swatch, country of origin, and storage.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:16.692
There is a outline, uh, right
now on color swatch, which,

00:09:16.768 --> 00:09:18.659
uh, is a basic black outline.

00:09:18.663 --> 00:09:22.784
It does show, uh, the user's
current location or current focus

00:09:22.786 --> 00:09:24.248
indicator where they are on.

00:09:25.991 --> 00:09:30.358
Now, a couple of different examples
wherein the color contrast fails.

00:09:30.677 --> 00:09:34.256
So we have a e-commerce
website example here.

00:09:34.691 --> 00:09:41.539
Uh, here we have a focus indicator, uh,
which, uh, against the f- uh, festive

00:09:41.561 --> 00:09:46.390
background kind of, uh, fails the
requirement, which are meant to be there

00:09:46.392 --> 00:09:50.731
for, uh, 1.4.11 or non-text contrast.

00:09:51.069 --> 00:09:55.976
So this focus indicator, uh, again,
users may be able to track themselves

00:09:55.976 --> 00:10:00.550
sometimes, but at times, uh, across
different backgrounds, they might

00:10:00.587 --> 00:10:02.563
lose, uh, track of themselves.

00:10:02.563 --> 00:10:06.398
They might end up, uh, somewhere they
didn't really predict they would be.

00:10:06.815 --> 00:10:10.604
So this example, the foreground
and the background comes up to a

00:10:10.607 --> 00:10:14.139
color contrast ratio of 1.503:1.

00:10:14.613 --> 00:10:21.438
And again, the implementation is not
bad, but it is not fully thought of

00:10:21.616 --> 00:10:27.060
or tested against different variety of
colors that will be used on the website

00:10:30.316 --> 00:10:37.232
Uh, we do have a demo of our product, uh,
which will be taken over, uh, by Pranali.

00:10:37.777 --> 00:10:41.093
But we will be demoing… I'll
give you a gist of what we

00:10:41.099 --> 00:10:42.570
are going to show you next.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:47.882
Uh, there will be a demo of color
contrast analyzer and a keyboard demo

00:10:47.978 --> 00:10:52.497
wherein we can track, uh, the keyboard
using the Forced Focus bookmarklet.

00:10:53.031 --> 00:10:56.179
Uh, it'll basically be a
demo on live web pages.

00:10:56.202 --> 00:10:57.732
So yeah, over to you, Pranali.

00:10:58.613 --> 00:11:03.838
So I'll be demonstrating the color
contrast focus outline, how we

00:11:03.857 --> 00:11:07.656
can check using the BarrierBreak
tool that is A11yInspect.

00:11:08.089 --> 00:11:14.593
And for that, I am demonstrating, uh,
the example from the Amazon.in, where

00:11:14.594 --> 00:11:20.194
you all can see there's a sign-in
form, and we are having gray color

00:11:20.243 --> 00:11:22.333
outline around the Continue button.

00:11:23.271 --> 00:11:30.854
So I'm doing Inspect Element, and here
we have an option that is A11yInspect.

00:11:32.789 --> 00:11:39.652
If you scroll down, we will get contrast
checker at the sidebar, where it will

00:11:39.713 --> 00:11:42.938
allow user to pick the contrast ratio.

00:11:43.445 --> 00:11:48.967
So for now, I'm just trying to pick the
foreground color that is for the outline,

00:11:49.367 --> 00:11:56.396
which is in a gray, and then I will select
background color that is in a white.

00:11:57.372 --> 00:12:02.648
Furthermore, I'll just scroll down, and
we will see the focus outline that is,

00:12:02.848 --> 00:12:11.163
uh, focus outline color contrast ratio
that is coming as 1.74:1, which is not

00:12:11.193 --> 00:12:13.898
actually meeting the WCAG standard.

00:12:14.523 --> 00:12:21.799
When there is a non-contrast text, uh, the
color contrast ratio needs to meet as 3:1.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:28.339
If you scroll down, we have
also the focus ratio mentioned.

00:12:28.503 --> 00:12:34.591
That is minimum ratio,
uh, 3:1, 4.5:1, and 7:1.

00:12:35.272 --> 00:12:39.218
So for now, I'm keeping
myself selected for the 3:1.

00:12:40.356 --> 00:12:46.025
Furthermore, if we see, there are some
suggestions provided by the A11yInspect

00:12:46.079 --> 00:12:52.978
where it will be helpful for the developer
team to pick the appropriate color shade

00:12:53.147 --> 00:12:56.157
so that it will pass the requirement.

00:12:59.065 --> 00:13:04.410
Now we will see another demo that
is for the keyboard interaction.

00:13:06.025 --> 00:13:12.076
So here we will try to understand, uh,
the impact on the user and not only

00:13:12.077 --> 00:13:14.874
focused on the accessibility failures.

00:13:15.449 --> 00:13:21.728
So I will try to navigate the entire page
using the Tab key, where you will able

00:13:21.735 --> 00:13:26.558
to see some of the interactive elements
are having keyboard focus outline,

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:31.740
and some of the interactive elements
are not receiving the keyboard focus.

00:13:32.158 --> 00:13:36.141
And then we will see how
A11yInspect will help us out

00:13:36.613 --> 00:13:39.213
to locate ourself where we are.

00:13:48.953 --> 00:13:54.291
So some of the focus, uh, we can see
present around the interactive elements,

00:13:54.962 --> 00:13:57.267
and I'm moving further using Tab key.

00:13:59.969 --> 00:14:05.823
And now here below the Shop all
things Home, there are interactive

00:14:05.862 --> 00:14:10.724
controls that are Living Room,
Bedroom, Dining Room, et cetera.

00:14:11.078 --> 00:14:14.692
So those controls are not
taking keyboard focus outline.

00:14:15.623 --> 00:14:17.342
Let's press Tab key

00:14:20.132 --> 00:14:25.791
As a keyboard user, I cannot see my
focus around those control, but the next

00:14:25.808 --> 00:14:28.474
further controls are keyboard focusable.

00:14:29.159 --> 00:14:33.666
Now let me just quickly turn on the
bookmarklet, which is present in

00:14:33.898 --> 00:14:36.864
A11yInspect, that is Forced Focus.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:43.106
This will help us out to
locate our focus on the website

00:14:47.999 --> 00:14:53.347
As you can see, same controls
when I turn on the bookmarklet

00:14:53.351 --> 00:14:55.332
options are getting focusable

00:15:00.829 --> 00:15:06.171
So this is helpful for us to
understand where our focus is moving.

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:12.625
I'll stop my screen now and present
my PPT for further presentation.

00:15:14.342 --> 00:15:18.966
So we will move further with our
next pillar, that is predictability.

00:15:20.083 --> 00:15:25.718
Now, as Raj already mentioned, like
what exactly the predictability is.

00:15:26.163 --> 00:15:29.589
User should be anticipate
where their focus will move.

00:15:30.238 --> 00:15:35.530
When there's unexpected changes
happen, user might question themselves

00:15:35.584 --> 00:15:40.319
whether the action taken from the user
end, that is correct or incorrect.

00:15:41.326 --> 00:15:46.774
They might think about, "Did I press
any wrong key? Did I break something?

00:15:47.216 --> 00:15:53.208
Or why this end up here?" So instead
of asking question by user, we need

00:15:53.249 --> 00:15:59.026
to ask some question from our end
while designing the focus management.

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:04.526
So that question would be,
should this be focusable?

00:16:05.214 --> 00:16:08.680
Did I make a mistake
or was the focus clear?

00:16:09.319 --> 00:16:12.702
Did I just expand
something or open a pop-up?

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:16.108
Why this is able focus it?

00:16:18.039 --> 00:16:21.127
We will try to understand
using some of the example.

00:16:21.909 --> 00:16:27.593
Now, not every element needs to have
focus unless those are interactive.

00:16:28.151 --> 00:16:33.925
So making element focusable which
are unnecessary, for example, the

00:16:33.925 --> 00:16:39.150
textual content or maybe the decorative
images, which is increasing user

00:16:39.155 --> 00:16:41.550
efforts and creating confusion.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:46.810
So we will try to understand
using the example, and our

00:16:46.817 --> 00:16:49.414
first example, that is dialog.

00:16:49.991 --> 00:16:52.289
So focus moves behind the dialog.

00:16:52.768 --> 00:16:57.405
User expect that focus should
move to the filter dialog.

00:16:58.622 --> 00:17:03.314
As a user, if the focus is not
moving appropriately where they are

00:17:03.338 --> 00:17:08.406
expecting, that's something, again,
challenging for user to navigate

00:17:08.432 --> 00:17:14.670
within the user interface, where
they are interacting with unnecessary

00:17:14.845 --> 00:17:20.225
or hidden background content if the
focus management handled incorrectly

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:27.474
In this particular example, you
can see I have just trigger filter

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:32.591
button where we can see the apply
filters dialog, and still my focus

00:17:32.664 --> 00:17:35.818
is about the, uh, background content.

00:17:36.246 --> 00:17:40.906
So this is not something expected
behavior when it comes to a dialog

00:17:40.908 --> 00:17:45.738
example where we are expecting the
focus should be handled properly.

00:17:47.397 --> 00:17:52.004
Another example I would like to
demonstrate that is, uh, focus

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:54.031
does not move to the form control.

00:17:54.334 --> 00:17:59.812
As you can see here, there is a
small form, and when user press a

00:17:59.814 --> 00:18:04.309
continue button, we are expecting
that focus should be moved to

00:18:04.322 --> 00:18:07.217
the first invalid form controls.

00:18:07.785 --> 00:18:12.328
Or at least there should be an
indication for user to indicate

00:18:12.386 --> 00:18:16.467
what exactly went wrong while
they are continuing with the form

00:18:21.252 --> 00:18:26.233
Another example here I would like to
explain about the dynamic changes.

00:18:26.824 --> 00:18:31.806
So once there's a dynamic changes
happening within the user interface,

00:18:32.171 --> 00:18:36.526
or let's say there are some kind
of search results or something

00:18:36.552 --> 00:18:38.880
is adding as a new content.

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:45.808
So definitely instead of moving, uh, focus
manually by user, we would expect that

00:18:46.009 --> 00:18:52.127
focus should be set while designing the
focus management so that user no need to

00:18:52.506 --> 00:18:55.353
manually jump to the newly added content.

00:18:55.914 --> 00:19:00.048
So here I have taken example where
you can see the read more button is

00:19:00.067 --> 00:19:04.849
present, and once user press that
read more button, the more content

00:19:04.864 --> 00:19:06.545
will get appears on the screen.

00:19:07.083 --> 00:19:13.389
So definitely the expectation is something
to set the focus directly to the newly

00:19:13.389 --> 00:19:18.826
appearing content so that user can
continue their interaction smoothly.

00:19:20.849 --> 00:19:24.871
Now we will move to our next
pillar, that is control.

00:19:26.443 --> 00:19:31.057
This is our final pillar where we
are, uh, trying to understand what

00:19:31.057 --> 00:19:33.659
exactly the third pillar is meant for.

00:19:34.147 --> 00:19:38.768
So control is about giving user
confidence and they… so that

00:19:38.788 --> 00:19:41.579
they can navigate and recover back.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:48.182
User should able to interact back and
forth within the interactive controls.

00:19:49.262 --> 00:19:51.495
What I am interacting with?

00:19:51.967 --> 00:19:53.698
Where do I move next?

00:19:54.196 --> 00:19:56.763
How do I get back to where I was?

00:19:57.208 --> 00:20:00.833
And did the form submitted
or was there any error?

00:20:03.166 --> 00:20:08.575
So now I would like to hand over to
Raj to explain you about the control

00:20:08.631 --> 00:20:14.997
pillars example So when you talk about
controls, uh, you are basically trying

00:20:15.013 --> 00:20:18.996
to understand, uh, how the control,
how the, uh, functionality would

00:20:18.997 --> 00:20:21.174
behave, where the focus will end up.

00:20:21.592 --> 00:20:26.029
Now, we have an example on screen, which
is a drag and drop sort of an example.

00:20:26.332 --> 00:20:29.461
You are able to create, uh, a
flowchart using the different

00:20:29.475 --> 00:20:30.694
nodes that are available.

00:20:31.121 --> 00:20:37.763
Here, users can navigate and can add the
input nodes or the default node or output

00:20:37.763 --> 00:20:42.480
node, whatever they feel like to the
canvas that they are interacting with.

00:20:42.765 --> 00:20:46.784
But once they do that, their
focus might end up somewhere else.

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:48.613
So why is that happening?

00:20:48.924 --> 00:20:53.581
Uh, it is primarily happening because
while designing or while developing,

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:57.984
it was not thought through where the
focus would end up, how the users

00:20:57.985 --> 00:21:02.037
will navigate and try to understand
whether users can get back to the

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.693
control they were interacting with.

00:21:04.063 --> 00:21:10.497
So this basically amounts to lack of,
uh, understanding of how basically

00:21:10.499 --> 00:21:15.852
focus management works, or it may
be a result of a poor planning.

00:21:16.573 --> 00:21:18.565
Again, this was one of the examples.

00:21:18.874 --> 00:21:23.348
Similar to, uh, what we have seen
before, we have another filter dialog.

00:21:23.736 --> 00:21:28.455
But what happens here is users are
trying to interact with the control.

00:21:28.671 --> 00:21:31.102
They are trying to open the filter dialog.

00:21:31.501 --> 00:21:35.068
Uh, maybe they have already done,
uh, with applying the filters, and

00:21:35.069 --> 00:21:36.588
they now want to close the dialog.

00:21:36.850 --> 00:21:40.953
But whenever users are trying to do
the same, they are not really able to

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:45.553
understand whether their focus will move
back to the correct location or not.

00:21:46.709 --> 00:21:50.190
For example, I was just
interacting witH1 of the products.

00:21:50.648 --> 00:21:54.140
I want similar type of products,
but I have opened this filter.

00:21:54.328 --> 00:21:59.053
I have applied the regular filters that
I want to be able to view a certain

00:21:59.068 --> 00:22:00.763
category, certain brand of products.

00:22:01.047 --> 00:22:03.727
Now I want to exit this
All filters dialog.

00:22:04.346 --> 00:22:05.545
Uh, what happens next?

00:22:05.714 --> 00:22:11.515
I close the dialog, and I end up somewhere
on top of the screen, or maybe where

00:22:11.517 --> 00:22:13.444
I don't really predict I would end up.

00:22:13.871 --> 00:22:17.211
So this is something that was
not expected, and users might

00:22:17.211 --> 00:22:19.059
get confused at times like this.

00:22:19.444 --> 00:22:27.108
So this is one of the primary examples
and most commonly seen implementations,

00:22:27.481 --> 00:22:32.126
uh, when it comes to dialog or when
it comes to similar type of controls

00:22:32.231 --> 00:22:34.097
which, uh, redirect focus also.

00:22:35.598 --> 00:22:40.010
Another type of example can be
with regards to different controls

00:22:40.212 --> 00:22:44.357
and how they are located or
situated on the, uh, web pages.

00:22:44.652 --> 00:22:50.640
So we do have one of the examples wherein
the focus order is weird just because

00:22:50.935 --> 00:22:55.813
there are some additional controls that
the developer wants to make interactive.

00:22:56.412 --> 00:23:01.688
Now here, uh, your focus jumps from
one place to another quite randomly.

00:23:01.713 --> 00:23:05.522
Users are not able to predict
where they'll end up, and because

00:23:05.523 --> 00:23:07.827
of which there will be a chaos.

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:10.586
There will be problems
that users will face.

00:23:11.123 --> 00:23:16.933
Uh, owing to that, we are using,
again, A11yInspects, one of the

00:23:16.935 --> 00:23:20.740
bookmarklets under A11yInspect, which
is Tab Order, to understand how it

00:23:20.763 --> 00:23:23.038
works or where the focus will end up.

00:23:23.461 --> 00:23:26.532
We'll be showing you a
demo on that shortly.

00:23:27.305 --> 00:23:32.712
Basically, the demo will consist of,
uh, how to use, uh, some bookmarklets

00:23:32.721 --> 00:23:36.245
to understand whether or not your
focus is moving to certain area.

00:23:36.736 --> 00:23:41.413
It can be dialogues, it can be
controls, uh, which are not really

00:23:41.413 --> 00:23:42.945
predictable would receive focus.

00:23:44.735 --> 00:23:44.933
Yep.

00:23:45.246 --> 00:23:52.062
So we are looking at the example for this
website, which lets you read articles.

00:23:52.779 --> 00:23:58.878
Uh, we'll try and understand how the focus
moves here using the Tab Order bookmarklet

00:24:01.747 --> 00:24:06.040
So the bookmarklet does identify
where and how the focus will move.

00:24:06.531 --> 00:24:11.615
Uh, predictably so, it jumps from
the breadcrumb regions or the

00:24:11.616 --> 00:24:17.686
filters that are available to random
links, and then it moves to the

00:24:17.686 --> 00:24:23.158
tab controls that are available to
change the content on the screen.

00:24:23.869 --> 00:24:25.924
So I can move from one place to another.

00:24:26.494 --> 00:24:31.089
And again, these controls
are placed at random.

00:24:31.425 --> 00:24:37.185
They receive focus, uh, in a very
different manner than what is expected.

00:24:37.472 --> 00:24:39.864
So this can create confusion for people.

00:24:40.754 --> 00:24:43.588
I'll quickly jump into one
of the good examples that we

00:24:43.624 --> 00:24:46.580
think should be demonstrated.

00:24:46.984 --> 00:24:53.248
I'll quickly open, uh, one of the filters
or filter dialogues over on this website.

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:58.456
Now, I have a price filter, which
again is a multi-thumb slider.

00:24:59.041 --> 00:25:01.176
I'm just simply applying the

00:25:03.707 --> 00:25:09.531
Tab order bookmarklet, and
it is much more predictable.

00:25:09.561 --> 00:25:12.467
For example, users… Okay.

00:25:13.443 --> 00:25:17.260
I think we just close the… Yeah.

00:25:17.856 --> 00:25:23.199
So if we, again, uh, want to
recreate the same, would go to and

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:25.736
apply the tab order bookmarklet.

00:25:27.328 --> 00:25:34.181
And from here we can understand that our
focus, uh, ended up on the price control,

00:25:34.632 --> 00:25:41.354
after which it moved in the price category
or price filter pop-up that was there.

00:25:41.921 --> 00:25:46.257
From there, we are able to navigate
between the controls, uh, which

00:25:46.258 --> 00:25:52.077
are the slider headers and out
of the pop-up, and then move on

00:25:52.108 --> 00:25:54.485
to in a more predictable manner.

00:25:55.023 --> 00:25:58.707
So this was one of the examples
that, uh, we had planned.

00:25:59.324 --> 00:26:00.963
Uh, we'll quickly

00:26:03.011 --> 00:26:07.253
Go into one of the different,
uh, implementations wherein it

00:26:07.316 --> 00:26:10.224
is not quite what it is expected.

00:26:10.686 --> 00:26:14.529
So what we'll do here is we'll
trigger the More Filters control

00:26:15.537 --> 00:26:18.066
and jump into the bookmarklet again.

00:26:18.814 --> 00:26:26.885
Here, the bookmarklet does show a path,
but, uh, you might notice that I have

00:26:26.899 --> 00:26:32.053
opened the Apply Filter dialog, which
doesn't really receive any keyboard focus.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:37.394
So this is one of the examples wherein
couple of pillars do come in handy.

00:26:37.712 --> 00:26:42.051
So second, uh, pillar, which is
predictability, and third pillar,

00:26:42.056 --> 00:26:46.275
which is control, are really missing
in this scenario, because users

00:26:46.277 --> 00:26:50.539
are not at all able to navigate to
this dialog or interact with any

00:26:50.539 --> 00:26:52.246
of the controls that are present.

00:26:53.041 --> 00:26:56.203
So there are a few controls here
which receive keyboard focus.

00:26:56.515 --> 00:27:00.046
Some of them do, but there
is no focus indicator again.

00:27:00.514 --> 00:27:03.215
So we'll try and predict
our way out of this.

00:27:03.261 --> 00:27:04.864
I guess we'll end up somewhere.

00:27:04.868 --> 00:27:06.641
Okay, we are on a product now.

00:27:07.138 --> 00:27:09.930
So this, you can see,
is really unpredictable.

00:27:10.142 --> 00:27:15.919
I'll move back to the filters, see if
I'm able to interact with those now.

00:27:16.323 --> 00:27:16.557
Yep.

00:27:18.132 --> 00:27:20.009
I did activate the filters.

00:27:20.068 --> 00:27:20.904
Let me see.

00:27:22.623 --> 00:27:24.244
Okay, still stumbling around.

00:27:25.186 --> 00:27:29.726
Okay, now that I see that my focus
is still in the background, I am not

00:27:29.744 --> 00:27:31.823
really interacting with my filters.

00:27:32.322 --> 00:27:33.882
This can happen at times.

00:27:33.918 --> 00:27:38.765
The filters might be available
at end of the page, so that is

00:27:38.765 --> 00:27:41.916
something, uh, that one might expect.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:47.874
But this is not really a good experience
or good user experience per se.

00:27:48.301 --> 00:27:51.809
Uh, if I'm able to interact
witH1 of the controls, do I end

00:27:51.819 --> 00:27:53.685
up somewhere on the filters?

00:27:55.453 --> 00:27:56.244
Again, no.

00:27:57.001 --> 00:28:03.475
So this kind of an implementation
may create confusion for users and,

00:28:03.643 --> 00:28:10.035
uh, is really bad for keyboard users
especially, uh, who primarily rely on

00:28:10.041 --> 00:28:16.008
a focus indicator to tell whether they
are interacting with proper control that

00:28:16.023 --> 00:28:17.466
they expect to interact with or not.

00:28:18.737 --> 00:28:24.962
Uh, when you are interacting with the
menus or the navigation menus, you

00:28:24.996 --> 00:28:30.644
can open search at random, and you
have to navigate through the search

00:28:33.059 --> 00:28:38.058
Not the controls actually, but you'll
end up somewhere out of the header.

00:28:38.838 --> 00:28:42.226
Now I'm back on the table,
uh, with the page navigation

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:45.081
headers and back to the filters.

00:28:45.496 --> 00:28:48.111
So yeah, that was kind of confusing.

00:28:48.362 --> 00:28:53.270
I'm sure it was confusing for everybody
who is attending the session as well.

00:28:54.993 --> 00:28:59.631
Here we are looking at, uh, how
a good example would look like.

00:28:59.988 --> 00:29:02.508
We are interacting with
the e-commerce website.

00:29:04.347 --> 00:29:11.089
I'm able to navigate to controls,
uh, which I think I'm going to need,

00:29:11.366 --> 00:29:13.249
uh, to add my products to cart.

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:17.880
Now I'm on filters, expanding the filters

00:29:20.872 --> 00:29:21.244
Okay.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.215
I can navigate to the filters.

00:29:23.217 --> 00:29:24.622
I can change the price.

00:29:25.372 --> 00:29:28.086
I'll close the filters by pressing Escape.

00:29:29.282 --> 00:29:31.198
Still navigating easily.

00:29:31.445 --> 00:29:34.310
Let me see if I clear up the
filters, where do I end up?

00:29:36.304 --> 00:29:40.667
Okay, that is something that
can be problematic for users.

00:29:41.129 --> 00:29:47.302
I just clicked Clear All, and I ended
up on the top of the page rather

00:29:47.307 --> 00:29:52.511
than going to one of the filters
that was maybe activated earlier.

00:29:53.719 --> 00:29:58.829
If the control gets disappeared, at
times, uh, people will implement this

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:04.555
kind of, uh, a gateway wherein the
focus will just move to top of the

00:30:04.555 --> 00:30:08.015
page or to a random location at times.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:12.754
Uh, let's take a look at the menus

00:30:18.299 --> 00:30:18.503
Yeah.

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:21.304
So the menu's predictably received focus

00:30:27.178 --> 00:30:30.452
So that would be some
of the good examples.

00:30:30.485 --> 00:30:34.803
One of, uh, clear all might
not sit well with everybody.

00:30:36.511 --> 00:30:40.466
So with that, uh, that
was end of the demo.

00:30:41.533 --> 00:30:41.797
Yep.

00:30:42.372 --> 00:30:48.257
So that was a short demo on how a11y can
help you identify where your focus would

00:30:48.257 --> 00:30:51.371
move and does it move predictably or not.

00:30:51.927 --> 00:30:55.520
Again, that is something that
can be done using other tools.

