00:35:06.747 --> 00:35:08.495
telling them about accessibility.

00:35:08.496 --> 00:35:11.429
This is a new thing
that you have to do now.

00:35:11.474 --> 00:35:18.173
Uh, that is where basically, uh, making
them understand how this affects users.

00:35:18.449 --> 00:35:24.819
Uh, not only keyboard users, somebody
like a person with low vision, right?

00:35:24.885 --> 00:35:30.846
A person, uh, who is again reliant on
a keyboard for situational reasons.

00:35:30.868 --> 00:35:32.687
Maybe they're not able to use a mouse.

00:35:32.712 --> 00:35:34.499
Maybe their trackpad stopped working.

00:35:34.902 --> 00:35:37.512
How do they w- interact
with the website, right?

00:35:37.835 --> 00:35:41.801
So that- Okay … is where, like,
putting things into perspective, putting

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:49.133
things, uh- Like, uh, wearing the
user's shoes, I would say, in a way.

00:35:49.504 --> 00:35:52.246
Uh, I'm not sure I put it correctly there.

00:35:52.267 --> 00:35:57.518
Like, uh, that would be something that
people should be aware of, should be made

00:35:57.538 --> 00:36:02.445
aware of, rather, uh, when you're dealing
with, uh, this type of a conversation.

00:36:02.472 --> 00:36:07.950
And generally explaining something,
uh, straight away with like, "This

00:36:07.961 --> 00:36:11.316
is the criteria, just fulfill
that criteria," is not enough.

00:36:11.653 --> 00:36:13.309
Uh, they need- Mm-hmm … to step up.

00:36:13.331 --> 00:36:17.186
They need to try and understand
in the user perspective and how

00:36:17.231 --> 00:36:22.108
and what that, uh, implementation
is doing to user experience.

00:36:23.106 --> 00:36:25.180
Chris Hinds: So you're showing
