00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:31.145
I can see how that would
really build some empathy.

00:36:31.385 --> 00:36:32.437
Thank you for that answer.

00:36:32.932 --> 00:36:36.822
Uh, all right, so next question, also from
an anonymous attendee: Should designers

00:36:36.823 --> 00:36:41.851
and developers customize the focus outline
or leave the browser default as it is?

00:36:43.818 --> 00:36:44.985
Raj Rajeshirke: Parnali,
you want to take this one?

00:36:45.617 --> 00:36:46.399
Pranali Bane: Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:36:46.482 --> 00:36:46.711
Raj Rajeshirke: Yeah.

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:51.104
Pranali Bane: So, uh, best practice
and best approach to go with the

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:53.476
default fo- browser focus outline.

00:36:54.019 --> 00:36:59.075
Uh, if in case just because of the, uh,
background content your, uh, default

00:36:59.085 --> 00:37:04.441
focus outline is not clearly visible,
definitely in that particular scenario

00:37:04.456 --> 00:37:09.635
we… developer can use the, uh, custom
outline, but they also need to make

00:37:09.638 --> 00:37:15.103
sure, uh, they have to meet the, uh,
color contrast requirement, that is 3:1.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:16.513
Chris Hinds: Mm-hmm.

00:37:17.421 --> 00:37:17.749
Okay.

00:37:18.062 --> 00:37:21.550
So it's okay to do customized,
uh, focus outlines, but they

00:37:21.552 --> 00:37:22.778
have to meet color contrast?

00:37:22.778 --> 00:37:23.137
Pranali Bane: Yeah.

00:37:23.463 --> 00:37:24.341
Chris Hinds: Definitely.

00:37:24.341 --> 00:37:24.351
Raj Rajeshirke: Consistently.

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:24.894
Chris Hinds: Yeah.

00:37:24.894 --> 00:37:25.247
Okay.

00:37:25.426 --> 00:37:25.800
Great.

00:37:26.492 --> 00:37:29.107
Raj Rajeshirke: Um- There are so
many requirements related to that.

00:37:30.383 --> 00:37:33.204
Some of them are mentioned
in the chat, some of them are

00:37:33.212 --> 00:37:35.387
mentioned in the slides, but yeah.

00:37:35.708 --> 00:37:40.159
Uh, something like, you know, a
custom focus indicator is fine unless

00:37:40.159 --> 00:37:46.667
and until you are going, uh, way
out of, uh, the normal and going

00:37:46.667 --> 00:37:48.452
crazy about that focus indicator.

00:37:48.461 --> 00:37:49.140
That is fine.

00:37:49.552 --> 00:37:53.681
But yeah, make sure to at least meet
the minimum criteria, which is color

00:37:53.683 --> 00:37:57.320
contrast for non-text content, 3.0:1.

00:37:57.792 --> 00:38:00.071
And yeah, customize it as you want.

00:38:01.297 --> 00:38:01.711
Chris Hinds: Great.

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:07.335
And another anonymous attendee asks,
for clickable product cards, so in my

00:38:07.335 --> 00:38:12.741
head I'm envisioning, you know, a grid
of tiles, uh, where should focus land,

00:38:12.755 --> 00:38:15.283
the title link or the entire card?

00:38:15.294 --> 00:38:17.582
What's your professional opinion on that?

00:38:19.093 --> 00:38:22.411
Raj Rajeshirke: Uh, cards as
in the cards that flip, right?

00:38:22.438 --> 00:38:26.693
So I'm taking an example wherein
may- there may be a person, there is

00:38:26.697 --> 00:38:30.694
a photo of a person and, uh, their
name, for example, their designation.

00:38:31.004 --> 00:38:36.029
When you activate, uh, like when you
try to flip that card, uh, it flips

00:38:36.029 --> 00:38:40.154
into, like revealing their whole
bio or something related to that.

00:38:40.672 --> 00:38:41.015
Uh-

00:38:41.044 --> 00:38:41.314
Pranali Bane: Mm-hmm

00:38:41.352 --> 00:38:44.962
…  Raj Rajeshirke: so in scenarios
like those, I prefer having a

00:38:44.965 --> 00:38:50.051
dedicated button, uh, which you know
becomes available on focus, uh, or

00:38:50.093 --> 00:38:54.347
keyboard focus, and keeping the same
functionality available for mouse.

00:38:54.359 --> 00:38:56.520
So mouse users are not really affected.

00:38:56.535 --> 00:38:59.795
They can click anywhere and
the card will flip and unflip.

00:39:00.167 --> 00:39:04.846
Uh, but if, uh, dealing with, you
know, keyboards, uh, issues and

00:39:04.846 --> 00:39:08.687
dealing with, uh, screen reader
related issues, that is primary…

00:39:08.719 --> 00:39:12.663
my primary go-to, like provide
a dedicated button on both

00:39:12.663 --> 00:39:14.238
ends, flip and unflip button.

00:39:14.297 --> 00:39:16.806
Make it available on
keyboard focus, that is fine.

00:39:17.243 --> 00:39:22.924
And if you just flip the card,
uh, your next logical focus would

00:39:22.948 --> 00:39:24.668
be on the unflip button, right?

00:39:24.995 --> 00:39:27.597
You may want to just read
and unflip that card again.

00:39:28.349 --> 00:39:30.169
So that's how I would tackle that.

00:39:30.169 --> 00:39:30.461
Yeah.

00:39:32.312 --> 00:39:33.183
Chris Hinds: All right.

00:39:34.095 --> 00:39:36.446
And we have another one from
an anonymous attendee here.

00:39:36.503 --> 00:39:41.640
Uh, when dealing with highly dynamic
backgrounds like a hero images or

00:39:41.685 --> 00:39:47.402
gradient buttons, what are your favorite
techniques for ensuring focus indicators

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:52.479
while maintaining that 3:1 contrast
ratio without compromising the design?

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:58.670
Raj Rajeshirke: So, uh, going for like…
Yeah, Pranali, sorry, take it ahead.

00:40:00.348 --> 00:40:00.718
Pranali Bane: Yeah.

00:40:00.802 --> 00:40:07.411
So, uh, as per my, um, experience, I
would, uh, suggest to avoid highly dynamic

00:40:07.432 --> 00:40:13.903
background, uh, because, uh, as per the
design, the how you make design in a

00:40:13.907 --> 00:40:20.355
simple and, um, you know, a very expected
contrast ratio would, uh, would be more,

00:40:20.754 --> 00:40:22.945
uh, beneficial for all user groups.

00:40:23.333 --> 00:40:29.156
But still, if it's the business decision
to have the dynamic background, uh, and

00:40:29.156 --> 00:40:33.523
then we need to check the focus outline,
definitely we have to stick with the WCAG

00:40:33.535 --> 00:40:36.248
success criteria requirement that is 3:1.

00:40:37.218 --> 00:40:37.679
Raj Rajeshirke: Yeah.

00:40:37.734 --> 00:40:39.358
Pranali Bane: Anything
that you would like to add?

00:40:39.425 --> 00:40:45.105
Raj Rajeshirke: So adding to that, like
if you want, uh, like dynamic content,

00:40:45.178 --> 00:40:50.505
gradient backgrounds, it is fine at times,
but there are also WCAG, uh, you know,

00:40:50.507 --> 00:40:53.054
techniques, not just success criterias.

00:40:53.056 --> 00:40:56.667
If you go into success criterias
and read about it, at the very

00:40:56.667 --> 00:40:58.582
bottom you'll find these techniques.

00:40:58.815 --> 00:41:03.623
So one of the techniques talks
about, uh, sandwiching couple of

00:41:03.623 --> 00:41:06.275
different layers for focus indicators.

00:41:06.531 --> 00:41:11.968
So you could have two different, uh,
contrasting colors, basically black and

00:41:11.968 --> 00:41:14.173
white as in like the default example.

00:41:14.580 --> 00:41:19.645
Uh, you can have that, and either
that's white inside, black outside.

00:41:19.645 --> 00:41:23.351
Like it acts like a, a
dynamic, uh, indicator.

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:25.853
Like it can pass against
lighter backgrounds.

00:41:25.857 --> 00:41:27.959
The black one can pass
against lighter backgrounds.

00:41:27.978 --> 00:41:29.972
White can pass against darker background.

00:41:30.191 --> 00:41:35.027
So that is again something that is, uh,
commonly seen and, uh, people should

00:41:35.028 --> 00:41:37.476
follow, ideally, in situations like this.

00:41:38.819 --> 00:41:39.071
Chris Hinds: Yeah.

00:41:39.071 --> 00:41:42.750
I've, I've seen that myself on really
design-heavy websites where it'll

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:44.972
be this big chunky focus indicator.

00:41:44.988 --> 00:41:45.329
Yeah.

00:41:45.337 --> 00:41:47.970
Chunky is the word I'm gonna use,
where it's like two different lines

00:41:47.970 --> 00:41:51.581
and the lines are pretty thick so that
it accounts for everything if people

00:41:51.581 --> 00:41:55.285
want this really variable, uh, design.

00:41:55.589 --> 00:41:56.428
Um, all right.

00:41:56.442 --> 00:41:56.792
Great.

00:41:56.836 --> 00:41:57.245
Great.

00:41:57.276 --> 00:41:58.247
Wonderful answer.

00:41:58.273 --> 00:41:58.946
Thank you.

00:41:59.393 --> 00:42:03.811
Uh, next one, also from an anonymous
attendee, uh, says, "I appreciate

00:42:03.811 --> 00:42:06.790
the demonstration of keyboard
testing." Uh, I presume that was

00:42:06.792 --> 00:42:08.380
the Walmart thing you were showing.

00:42:08.385 --> 00:42:08.598
Yeah.

00:42:08.598 --> 00:42:09.475
That was really cool.

00:42:09.883 --> 00:42:14.415
Uh, what are some areas of keyboard
testing that you can see amateur or that

00:42:14.415 --> 00:42:16.655
you see amateur testers struggle with?

00:42:17.046 --> 00:42:20.688
What are some things that people miss
frequently while keyboard testing?

00:42:22.711 --> 00:42:23.376
Raj Rajeshirke: Uh, Pranali?

00:42:24.902 --> 00:42:25.213
Pranali Bane: Yeah.

00:42:25.213 --> 00:42:25.223
Yeah.

00:42:25.669 --> 00:42:31.673
So, uh, the commonly, uh, we can see
there's a, uh… just because a focus

00:42:31.675 --> 00:42:36.186
is not visible, sometimes keyboard
testing gets skipped by the tester,

00:42:36.319 --> 00:42:41.680
or maybe, uh, the keyboard user are
getting skipped in the t- in terms

00:42:41.683 --> 00:42:43.464
of the development of the website.

00:42:44.167 --> 00:42:49.612
So, um, another thing that may
be, uh, the misconception about

00:42:49.627 --> 00:42:54.579
if the controls or interactive
elements are clickable using mouse.

00:42:54.588 --> 00:43:00.036
So maybe in that particular scenario,
we also need to consider that should we

00:43:00.038 --> 00:43:02.469
have the proper keyboard focus outline.

00:43:02.846 --> 00:43:06.373
And along with that, we also
need to take care about the,

00:43:06.412 --> 00:43:08.797
um, operability of the keyboard.

00:43:09.221 --> 00:43:13.499
There are standard keys that we can
use, that is Shift+Tab, Tab key,

00:43:13.933 --> 00:43:16.235
Spacebar, arrow keys and Enter key.

00:43:16.292 --> 00:43:21.512
So all tho- those are commonly used,
uh, key, uh, for the keyboard, uh,

00:43:21.907 --> 00:43:25.767
testing or a keyboard user that they
are using to navigate the website.

00:43:26.147 --> 00:43:30.249
So all those, uh, that we need to
take care while designing the website.

00:43:31.777 --> 00:43:32.266
Raj Rajeshirke: Yeah.

00:43:32.266 --> 00:43:32.295
All

00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:32.712
Chris Hinds: right.

00:43:33.238 --> 00:43:34.800
Raj Rajeshirke: So adding to
that point- Oh, please, Raj.

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:35.376
Sorry, yeah.

00:43:36.216 --> 00:43:40.574
Uh, adding to those points, like, good
call-out on, uh, what people miss.

00:43:40.603 --> 00:43:45.655
Like, generally, people assume if there's
no focus indicator, those controls

00:43:45.735 --> 00:43:47.691
are not really working for keyboard.

00:43:47.712 --> 00:43:47.724
Yeah.

00:43:47.729 --> 00:43:49.363
And they might flag an issue.

00:43:49.855 --> 00:43:50.154
Okay?

00:43:50.154 --> 00:43:50.420
Yeah.

00:43:50.456 --> 00:43:52.504
So that is, that is a common one.

00:43:52.568 --> 00:43:57.575
Uh, there are non-interactive elements,
like as in some text, random text is

00:43:57.604 --> 00:43:59.734
provided with tabindex=0 attribute.

00:44:00.015 --> 00:44:03.577
Now why that is, I'm not really
sure, but it is, like, a common

00:44:03.577 --> 00:44:05.385
occurrence when it comes to dialogues.

00:44:05.723 --> 00:44:10.204
Uh, when a dialogue pops up, people
generally provide a tabindex to

00:44:10.211 --> 00:44:14.448
the title or the container of that
dialogue, and that will be something

00:44:14.449 --> 00:44:16.794
that people miss most of the time.

00:44:17.334 --> 00:44:22.742
Uh, thirdly, I think, uh- Mm-hmm
… people will miss about keyboard

00:44:22.742 --> 00:44:28.837
issues is, uh, whether or not certain
functionalities actually perform… can

00:44:28.839 --> 00:44:30.643
be performed using keyboard shortcuts.

00:44:30.662 --> 00:44:33.853
So that is something that
people, uh, forgot to check for.

00:44:33.956 --> 00:44:37.006
So certainly website designers
and developers don't really,

00:44:37.145 --> 00:44:40.833
uh, mind, like, they don't give
a visual instruction for that.

00:44:40.836 --> 00:44:45.098
So again, that is, like, a blame
to share am- amongst users as

00:44:45.101 --> 00:44:46.895
well as, uh, the developers there.

00:44:46.926 --> 00:44:49.523
But yeah, that is
something that can be done.

00:44:49.537 --> 00:44:52.821
Like, you provide a keyboard
shortcut, also let people know

00:44:52.994 --> 00:44:54.683
about that keyboard shortcut, right?

00:44:54.813 --> 00:44:57.293
Uh, how else somebody else
is going to learn about it?

00:44:58.312 --> 00:44:58.677
Chris Hinds: Mm-hmm.

00:44:59.101 --> 00:44:59.333
Mm-hmm.

00:44:59.593 --> 00:45:00.179
Great answers.

00:45:00.182 --> 00:45:00.680
Thank you.

00:45:01.252 --> 00:45:01.732
All right.

00:45:01.931 --> 00:45:08.971
Um, Dave Miller asks, "What is the best
approach for established websites to

00:45:08.972 --> 00:45:10.728
improve accessibility, in your opinion?"

00:45:12.547 --> 00:45:15.962
Raj Rajeshirke: Uh, I would
say, uh, go with the basics.

00:45:16.124 --> 00:45:20.747
Uh, start with the, uh, things…
Start with things like checking

00:45:20.747 --> 00:45:23.026
for a heading structures, right?

00:45:23.114 --> 00:45:25.009
Are your headings in order, right?

00:45:25.019 --> 00:45:27.508
It starts with H1, goes up till H6.

00:45:27.849 --> 00:45:30.236
Are your sections making a lot of sense?

00:45:30.263 --> 00:45:31.629
Are your lists in order?

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:36.406
Uh, if you are using tables for layout,
which is very common across, you know,

00:45:36.410 --> 00:45:41.879
legacy websites, old websites which use
tables for layout purposes, uh, make sure

00:45:41.902 --> 00:45:48.306
that is, you know, either taken out of the
source code or if possible, just, uh, hide

00:45:48.319 --> 00:45:50.430
that using role presentation or something.

00:45:50.437 --> 00:45:53.361
Just make sure that the structure
doesn't get exposed because that's

00:45:53.658 --> 00:45:58.285
really not the thing that a user wants
to read every time they try to navigate.

00:45:58.663 --> 00:46:05.645
Uh, then you can look at color contrast
and, uh, other contrast related things.

00:46:05.658 --> 00:46:09.250
So go for color alone, which
is again, simple to fix.

00:46:09.250 --> 00:46:13.533
Like links are there, just provide
an underline, use CSS, right?

00:46:13.897 --> 00:46:16.236
Uh, that is something
that can be easily fixed.

00:46:16.247 --> 00:46:20.257
This improves your usability of
the website to at least twenty

00:46:20.261 --> 00:46:24.037
to thirty percent makes it better
than, uh, I would say close to fifty

00:46:24.037 --> 00:46:26.303
percent in that scenario, right?

00:46:26.495 --> 00:46:29.453
Somebody who is trying to interact
with the website will always

00:46:29.466 --> 00:46:32.107
look for, uh, these basic things.

00:46:32.426 --> 00:46:37.192
Then, uh, among the ones that we call
out in this session, uh, again, time and

00:46:37.192 --> 00:46:39.230
again, uh, would be focus indicators.

00:46:39.243 --> 00:46:41.261
Make sure the focus indicators are there.

00:46:41.839 --> 00:46:44.165
And yeah, couple of different things.

00:46:44.327 --> 00:46:46.451
Go for simple success criterias.

00:46:46.777 --> 00:46:50.962
Uh, but yeah, if there are any
blockers, definitely solve them.

00:46:51.156 --> 00:46:54.924
Solve those first 'cause, uh, those
are going to be a headache for

00:46:55.089 --> 00:46:56.558
people who are accessing your site

00:46:57.725 --> 00:46:57.948
Chris Hinds: Yeah.

00:46:58.266 --> 00:47:01.627
All right, we're gonna try to
do lightning round, um, because

00:47:01.629 --> 00:47:03.058
we've got two minutes left.

00:47:03.058 --> 00:47:07.066
So I'm gonna try to get through, uh,
as many questions as I can, and just

00:47:07.082 --> 00:47:09.761
try to give the shortest answer you
can so that we can get through these.

00:47:09.798 --> 00:47:14.319
Uh, Ka- Katie Tanner asks, "Do you
have any experience testing focus

00:47:14.319 --> 00:47:16.153
order with mobile applications?

00:47:16.170 --> 00:47:18.259
Are there any tools or
methods you would recommend?"

00:47:19.439 --> 00:47:20.837
Raj Rajeshirke: Yes, we do.

00:47:20.926 --> 00:47:25.960
And that would be like I would, uh,
in iOS it is ba- basically simple.

00:47:25.991 --> 00:47:29.138
Go and enable, uh, full keyboard access.

00:47:29.191 --> 00:47:29.714
That's it.

00:47:30.205 --> 00:47:32.336
Uh, for iOS it might be different.

00:47:32.688 --> 00:47:36.368
Uh, there will be grayed out
focus indicators on controls

00:47:36.368 --> 00:47:40.096
even in application and websites,
uh, that will create problems.

00:47:40.104 --> 00:47:43.234
But obviously, that is something
that, uh, can't be fixed.

00:47:43.281 --> 00:47:44.819
Android system works that way.

00:47:45.846 --> 00:47:46.214
Chris Hinds: Okay.

00:47:46.569 --> 00:47:46.857
Great.

00:47:46.873 --> 00:47:47.719
That was 30 seconds.

00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:48.283
All right, next one.

00:47:48.525 --> 00:47:52.470
Uh, Ed Harris asks, uh, "Sometimes
we have designs where the visual

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:55.796
design does not include a visible page
title, but we still need to provide a

00:47:55.797 --> 00:47:57.995
meaningful H1 for screen reader users.

00:47:58.458 --> 00:48:02.721
Um, i- is the approach of having a
visually hidden screen reader text

00:48:02.737 --> 00:48:07.307
H1 considered valid for WCAG 2.2 and
WordPress accessibility guidelines?

00:48:07.551 --> 00:48:11.019
If an automated accessibility tool reports
an issue with the hidden H1, should we

00:48:11.019 --> 00:48:14.649
change the markup or can a visually hidden
H1 be the correct semantic solution?"

00:48:15.763 --> 00:48:19.480
Raj Rajeshirke: Ideally, a hidden H1
won't be flagged by any automated tool.

00:48:19.567 --> 00:48:24.391
Uh, in scenarios it does, that's
probably a false positive.

00:48:24.415 --> 00:48:28.585
But I would recommend using a, a
off-screen heading, uh, whenever

00:48:28.613 --> 00:48:30.234
there is no visual heading present.

00:48:30.267 --> 00:48:31.310
That is totally fine.

00:48:31.703 --> 00:48:34.156
It doesn't really violate
any WCAG criterias.

00:48:35.130 --> 00:48:35.692
Chris Hinds: Thank you for that.

00:48:35.848 --> 00:48:38.861
All right, uh, Ashwini asks,
uh, "Elementor overrides a lot

00:48:38.886 --> 00:48:41.792
of accessibility fixes with its
wrapper and other methods. What's

00:48:41.792 --> 00:48:43.852
your take on this?" In 34 seconds.

00:48:46.047 --> 00:48:47.476
Raj Rajeshirke: Sorry,
can you repeat that?

00:48:47.861 --> 00:48:50.385
Chris Hinds: Uh, Elementor, the
WordPress page builder, overrides

00:48:50.387 --> 00:48:53.072
a lot of accessibility fixes with
its wrapper and other methods.

00:48:53.075 --> 00:48:58.893
So what's your take on Elementor or any
tool overriding accessibility fixes in 18

00:48:58.894 --> 00:48:59.247
Raj Rajeshirke: seconds?

00:48:59.250 --> 00:49:03.135
There's, there's, there's a comment,
"Don't use Ele- uh, Elementor." Uh, no.

00:49:03.354 --> 00:49:06.852
Uh, I, I am not that familiar
with WordPress and WordPress

00:49:06.854 --> 00:49:08.498
accessibility, but yeah.

00:49:08.701 --> 00:49:12.854
I would suggest, like, the most
common thing, don't use that.

00:49:13.735 --> 00:49:14.069
Chris Hinds: Okay.

00:49:14.316 --> 00:49:16.962
All right, and we're gonna have
to stop there 'cause that is time.

